   "source": [
    "### Sample_Penguins_regression"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "id": "f5ab88a0-15b2-4318-a0a6-7f2b7621efd8",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "\n",
    "# ✅ **1. Creating X and y**  \n",
    "### **What is X?**  \n",
    "- X (features) = all the input columns you use to *predict* something.  \n",
    "- Examples: bill_length_mm, species, island, flipper_length_mm…\n",
    "\n",
    "### **What is y?**  \n",
    "- y (target) = the column you want to **predict**.  \n",
    "- In class, y = `\"body_mass_g\"` (a number → regression).\n",
    "\n",
    "### **Why do we split into X and y?**  \n",
    "Because every supervised ML model needs:\n",
    "- **Inputs** → X  \n",
    "- **Output to learn from** → y  \n",
    "\n",
    "This matches the mathematical idea:  \n",
    "→ Model learns a function **f(X) ≈ y**\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "# ✅ **2. Train/Test Split**  \n",
    "### **What is it?**  \n",
    "A technique that divides data into:\n",
    "- **Training set (~80–70%)** → model learns patterns  \n",
    "- **Test set (~20–30%)** → model is evaluated on *unseen* data\n",
    "\n",
    "### **Why do we do it?**  \n",
    "To simulate real‑world prediction.  \n",
    "If we train and test on the same data, the model could memorize the answers (**overfitting**).  \n",
    "A separate test set checks if the model generalizes to unseen cases.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "# ✅ **3. Handling Missing Data (Dropping rows / Imputation)**  \n",
    "### **What is imputation?**  \n",
    "Replacing missing values with:\n",
    "- median (for numbers)  \n",
    "- most_frequent/mode (for categories)\n",
    "\n",
    "### **Why?**  \n",
    "ML models usually **cannot** train on missing values.  \n",
    "You either:\n",
    "1. Fill them in (impute), or  \n",
    "2. Drop those rows/columns  \n",
    "\n",
    "Your teacher showed:\n",
    "- Sometimes **dropping** is easier  \n",
    "- But better practice is often **imputing** with a model or statistics\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "# ✅ **4. Numeric & Categorical Features**  \n",
    "### **What are they?**  \n",
    "- **Numeric** = columns with numbers  \n",
    "- **Categorical** = columns with text labels (species, island, sex)\n",
    "\n",
    "### **Why separate them?**  \n",
    "Because different preprocessing is needed:\n",
    "- Numeric → median imputation, scaling  \n",
    "- Categorical → mode imputation, one-hot encoding  \n",
    "\n",
    "Models require all features to be **numeric** (especially AutoML systems).\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "# ✅ **5. One-Hot Encoding**  \n",
    "### **What is it?**  \n",
    "Transforms categories into 0/1 indicator columns.\n",
    "\n",
    "Example:  \n",
    "`species` → `species_Adelie`, `species_Chinstrap`, `species_Gentoo`\n",
    "\n",
    "### **Why?**  \n",
    "ML models cannot operate directly on text labels.  \n",
    "One‑hot encoding converts categories to usable numeric format.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "# ✅ **6. Dropping the First Dummy (`drop='first'`)**  \n",
    "### **What is it?**  \n",
    "Removes one dummy column during one-hot encoding.\n",
    "\n",
    "### **Why? (Regression reason)**  \n",
    "To avoid **multicollinearity**, where categories become perfectly redundant.  \n",
    "This prevents regression from becoming unstable.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "# ✅ **7. The Preprocessor (ColumnTransformer + Pipelines)**  \n",
    "### **What is it?**  \n",
    "A scikit‑learn object that:\n",
    "- Applies numeric transformations only to numeric columns  \n",
    "- Applies categorical transformations only to categorical columns  \n",
    "- Keeps everything organized\n",
    "\n",
    "### **Why do we use it?**  \n",
    "It ensures:\n",
    "- Clean workflow  \n",
    "- No mixing column types  \n",
    "- All preprocessing is done safely and reproducibly  \n",
    "- Avoids mistakes like scaling strings\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "# ✅ **8. Pipeline (sklearn’s Pipeline)**  \n",
    "### **What is it?**  \n",
    "A sequence:  \n",
    "`preprocessing → model`\n",
    "\n",
    "### **Why do we use it?**  \n",
    "- Keeps all steps together as a single object  \n",
    "- Prevents data leakage  \n",
    "- Makes your workflow clean:  \n",
    "  `pipe.fit(X_train, y_train)`  \n",
    "  `pipe.predict(X_test)`\n",
    "\n",
    "### **Important:**  \n",
    "Your teacher explains that **auto-sklearn is NOT pipeline-aware**, meaning you cannot pass the Pipeline directly to auto-sklearn.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "# ✅ **9. AutoML Not Being Pipeline-Aware**  \n",
    "### **What does “not pipeline-aware” mean?**  \n",
    "auto-sklearn:\n",
    "- Does **not** understand scikit‑learn Pipelines  \n",
    "- Does **not** run your preprocessing for you  \n",
    "- Expects **already clean, numeric arrays**\n",
    "\n",
    "### **Why is this important?**  \n",
    "It means you must:\n",
    "1. Preprocess using scikit‑learn manually  \n",
    "2. Transform X_train and X_test  \n",
    "3. Pass the **transformed arrays** into auto-sklearn\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "# ✅ **10. Manual Transformation (`preprocessor.transform`)**  \n",
    "### **What is it?**  \n",
    "You apply preprocessing *yourself*:\n",
    "\n",
    "```python\n",
    "preprocessor.fit(X_train)\n",
    "X_train_tr = preprocessor.transform(X_train)\n",
    "X_test_tr = preprocessor.transform(X_test)\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "### **Why do we do it?**  \n",
    "Because auto-sklearn cannot run the Pipeline internally.  \n",
    "So you prepare the data **before** sending it to the AutoML system.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "# ✅ **11. Fitting Preprocessing on Train Only (Leakage Prevention)**  \n",
    "### **What is data leakage?**  \n",
    "When information from the test set leaks into training by mistake.\n",
    "\n",
    "### **Why harmful?**  \n",
    "It inflates performance and makes your evaluation unrealistic.\n",
    "\n",
    "### **Correct workflow:**  \n",
    "```python\n",
    "preprocessor.fit(X_train)    # SAFE\n",
    "preprocessor.transform(X_train)\n",
    "preprocessor.transform(X_test)\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "# ✅ **12. Scalers (StandardScaler, MinMaxScaler, RobustScaler)**  \n",
    "\n",
    "### **StandardScaler (z-score)**  \n",
    "- Mean 0, std 1  \n",
    "- Sensitive to outliers  \n",
    "- Good for linear models, SVM, PCA\n",
    "\n",
    "### **MinMaxScaler (0 to 1)**  \n",
    "- Preserves distribution shape  \n",
    "- Very sensitive to outliers  \n",
    "- Good for neural nets and KNN\n",
    "\n",
    "### **RobustScaler (median + IQR)**  \n",
    "- Resistant to outliers  \n",
    "- Good for skewed or heavy-tailed data  \n",
    "\n",
    "### **Why did teacher remove scalers from his pipeline?**  \n",
    "Because auto-sklearn tests scalers automatically.  \n",
    "He didn’t want to “double-scale.”\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "# ✅ **13. AutoML (auto-sklearn) Searching for Best Model**  \n",
    "### **What AutoML does:**  \n",
    "- Tries multiple algorithms  \n",
    "- Tests multiple scalers  \n",
    "- Finds combinations  \n",
    "- Picks top performers  \n",
    "- Shows model leaderboard\n",
    "\n",
    "### **Why useful?**  \n",
    "It automates experimentation and helps select a good starting model for your final pipeline.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "# ✅ **14. Evaluating Model Performance (R², RMSE)**  \n",
    "### **R² (coefficient of determination)**  \n",
    "How much variability in y the model explains.\n",
    "\n",
    "### **RMSE (root mean squared error)**  \n",
    "Average size of prediction errors (same units as target).\n",
    "\n",
    "### **Why evaluate on test?**  \n",
    "To measure generalization, not memorization.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "# ✅ **15. Custom Cross-Validation (teacher’s manual approach)**  \n",
    "### **What is CV?**  \n",
    "Multiple train/test splits to measure stability of performance.\n",
    "\n",
    "### **Why do it?**  \n",
    "- Reduces randomness  \n",
    "- Shows consistency of model  \n",
    "- More trustworthy than a single train/test split\n",
    "\n",
    "Your teacher is working toward building a tool that does CV + AutoML + reporting."
   ]
